--- CHUNK 17 ---
Shusi uncovers actionable vulnerabilities of therapyresistant leukemia disease
Shusi also identified transcription factor RARA and protein tyrosine phosphatase PTPN6 (also known as SHP1) as promising targets to be combined with Ven/Aza. Retinoic acid receptor alpha (RARA) plays an important role in AML differentiation. The combination of the RARA agonist, tamibarotene, with chemotherapy drug Azacitdine (Tam/Aza) delivered a high complete remission rate and a rapid onset of response in RARA-positive newly diagnosed unfit AML patients 51 . Our study demonstrated that tamibarotene exhibits excellent ability to eradicate the monocytic subpopulation and has remarkable combinatorial effects with Ven/Aza, thereby providing preclinical evidence supporting the use of the successful Tam/Aza regimen to treat relapse/refractory responses to Ven/Aza (Fig. 6k). Src homology region 2 domain-containing phosphatase-1 (SHP1) is a member of the protein tyrosine phosphatase family and plays an important role in oncogenic signaling. A recent study in murine models of AML has demonstrated that SHP1 inhibition using SHP1 inhibitor TPI-1 or genetic approaches can target chemo-resistant leukemia stem cells through reactivation of metabolic pathways 52 . Our study compared the activity of SHP1 inhibitor TPI-1 and SHP1 agonist SC-43 and surprisingly found that the SHP1 agonist SC-43 but not inhibitor TPI-1 exhibited significant monocytic-specific killing and synergy with VA, suggesting the translational potential of this orally available SHP1 agonist (Fig. 6l). Notably, we verfied that these three Shusi-predicted targets (JAK2, RARA, and SHP1) were over-expressed in various genetic subtypes of human AML relative to normal hematopoietic stem and progenitor cells, suggesting their plausible therapeutic potential (Fig. S10). In conclusion, these results demonstrated the exciting potential of Shusi in designing novel effective drug combinations to address the clinically challenging developmental heterogeneity of human cancer.
